\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark3}{}{\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark4}{}{}}\textbf{Chapter
1}

\textbf{Introduction}
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark5}{}{}\textbf{Overview}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The purpose of the Rogue Device Detection System is to improve the
security of a Local Area Network (LAN) by detecting and controlling
devices that are not allowed to connect.

{[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark174}{1}{]} By detecting rogue devices and
ensuring they do not compromise network security, the Rogue Device
Detection System works to secure a Local Area Network (LAN). To make the
network secure and effective, it combines several techniques, including
actively scanning the network and verifying device manufacturer
information (OEM/OUI-based identification).

Python is used as the programming language and Django, which is a web
framework, is used to build the system. PostgreSQL Server is used for
data management to store and manage data about network activity and
trusted devices. Django ORM Model manages every interaction between the
system and the data, making data management simpler and more effective.
To ensure that it is both updated and user-friendly, the user interface
is designed using HTML templates and stylized with Tailwind CSS. The
network administra- tor may observe, administer, and control the network
in real time via the system's central dashboard.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark6}{}{}\textbf{Objectives}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
This project's primary objectives are to:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  Develop an effective system that can recognize and notify users of
  unauthorized devices connected to a local area network.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  Determine whether a device is authentic or not by using techniques
  such as OEM/OUI identification.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  Notify administrators in real time so they can take immediate action
  in the event of a security breach.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
1
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  Provide a user-friendly Administrator Dashboard that enables network
  administrators to generate statistics on network activities and
  control the whitelist of trusted devices.
\item
  Ensure that development is adaptive, flexible, and able to quickly add
  new features or adjustments as needed, by utilizing an Agile
  methodology.

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
  \item
    \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark7}{}{}\textbf{Problem Description}
  \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
Unauthorized devices that are able to connect to the network can pose
serious threats, such as data breaches or unauthorized access to
sensitive information. While many existing solutions primarily focus on
protecting wireless networks, leaving LANs vulnerable, the Rogue Device
Detection System aims to address this issue by developing a system
specifically designed to secure LANs. This is achieved by verifying
devices through OEM/OUI identification, active scanning, and central
management to handle alerts and take appropriate actions.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark8}{}{}\textbf{Methodology}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The Rogue Device Detection System is developed using the Agile
methodology, which breaks the project down into small, manageable tasks.
Each part delivers a portion of the system, and feedback from key users
(such as network administrators) is gathered at every stage to ensure
that the system meets user needs and can easily adapt to changes. The
Django framework serves as the system's foundation and supports
efficient and reliable backend processes, while PostgreSQL handles all
network device data. Nmap is used for network scanning to gather
information about all connected devices, and OEM/OUI verification is
utilized to determine whether a device is from a legitimate
manufacturer.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{2}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \emph{Project Scope} 3
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\includegraphics[width=3.86667in,height=3.45333in]{vertopal_a459ff205fbf487cb4aa404c5d4048f3/media/image2.jpeg}

\begin{quote}
\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark9}{}{}Figure 1.1: System Methodology
Workflow
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{3}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark10}{}{}\textbf{Project Scope}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
This project aims to improve the security of local area networks (LANs)
for businesses, educational institutions, and other establishments that
depend on reliable and safe network infrastructure. The project
specifically addresses the following topics:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Detection of Unauthorized Devices:} Locating rogue devices and
  immediately alerting administrators.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Manufacturer Information Verification:} Using MAC addresses
  and manufacturer information to confirm the legal status of devices.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Managing the Whitelist:} Providing administrators the
  resources they need to keep track of a list of authorized devices.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Alert and Reporting Features:} Enabling administrators to make
  decisions to safeguard the network using real-time alerts and regular
  reports.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Scalability:} Building a system that is scalable and
  adaptable, with the potential to incorporate machine learning in the
  future to enhance its capacity for detecting unauthorized devices.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{4}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark11}{}{}\textbf{Feasibility Study}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
Various factors, including technical capability, operational efficiency,
and security, were considered to assess the viability of the project.
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Technical Viability:} The project makes use of well-known
  technologies, such as Django, PostgreSQL, and Python, which have been
  proven reliable and effective for creating secure systems.
\item
  \textbf{Operational Feasibility:} Designed with simplicity in mind,
  the system includes a user-friendly dashboard that enables
  administrators to view and respond to alarms without requiring
  extensive technical knowledge.
\item
  \textbf{Security Considerations:} Security is of vital importance.
  PostgreSQL's secure handling of sensitive data capabilities ensures
  the integrity and safety of network data.
\end{itemize}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{5}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark12}{}{}\textbf{Areas of Application}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The Rogue Device Detection System benefits many industries requiring
secure LANs, including businesses and enterprises. By ensuring that only
authorized devices can connect to the network, it minimizes the risk of
data breaches. Specific areas of application include:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Educational Institutions:} Protecting university and school
  networks by preventing unauthorized access to critical data.
\item
  \textbf{Healthcare Facilities:} Safeguarding medical networks,
  ensuring that only approved medical devices can access sensitive
  patient data.
\item
  \textbf{Corporate Environments:} Enhancing security for enterprise
  networks by detecting and addressing rogue devices in real-time.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark13}{}{\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark14}{}{}}\textbf{Chapter
2}

\textbf{Literature Review}
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark15}{}{}\textbf{Introduction \& Evolution
  of Network Security}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
Network security used to be based on human audits and simple
authentication technologies, but as networks got bigger and more
complex, these methods soon became insufficient. In order to stay up
with the growing complexity of potential threats, network security has
changed over time to incorporate proactive detection approaches,
real-time analysis, and sophisticated monitoring tools.

The Rogue Device Detection System addresses the issue of rogue devices
in LAN networks by integrating several detection methods into a single,
seamless solution. The key modules are Network Scanning, which detects
all connected devices, OEM/OUI Iden- tification, which verifies the
legitimacy of device manufacturers, and Whitelist Verification, which
checks for authorization. Together, these modules offer a practical way
to guarantee that only devices with permission can connect to the
network{[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark175}{2}{]}.

The objective of this literature review is to demonstrate how the
suggested system better satisfies the needs of LAN environments than
conventional solutions by examining the development of these
technologies, current solutions, and the features of the system.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark16}{}{}\textbf{Techniques for Rogue
  Device Detection}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The combination of multiple methods, each addressing an individual
aspect of detecting unauthorized devices within a network, is necessary
for effective rogue device detection. Network scanning, OEM
verification, whitelist verification, device attribute validation,
Promiscuous mode, and alarm creation are some of the methods used in the
Rogue Device Detection System. When combined, these techniques offer a
thorough strategy for LAN environment security.

5
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark17}{}{\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark18}{}{}}\textbf{Network
  Scanning and Device Identification}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
Network scanning is the first step in the procedure, which finds every
device that is currently connected to the network. Nmap is used to find
devices and collect vital information, including IP addresses and MAC
addresses. An in-depth picture of linked devices can be obtained through
scanning using a variety of network protocols, including ICMP (Internet
Control Message Protocol), ARP, CDP (Cisco Discovery Protocol), and LLDP
(Link Layer Discovery Protocol).

By gathering information from several network stages, active scanning
with Nmap aids in creating a device inventory. For example, ICMP
determines if a device is reachable and operational, whereas ARP enables
the mapping of MAC addresses to IP addresses, offering further details
about the device's identity and network position.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark19}{}{}\textbf{Whitelist Comparison}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
When the devices are discovered, they are compared to a pre-made
whitelist in the asset in- ventory that includes the MAC addresses of
all devices that are trusted by the administrator. The whitelist module
shows all the trusted devices, and the network administrator ensures
that only devices that have been specifically approved are trusted.
Among the steps in the whitelist verification process are:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Comparison of MAC Addresses:} Each device's MAC address is
  compared against the whitelist to verify if it has been
  pre-authorized. If a device's MAC address does not match any on the
  whitelist, it is flagged as a potential rogue device.
\item
  \textbf{Threshold Validation:} Further checks are carried out to
  verify other attributes, including device type, operating system, and
  hostname. Even if a device meets the whitelist, it is marked for
  additional examination if the discovered properties are not within the
  anticipated range. This provides an extra level of analysis and aids
  in identifying potential compromises of authorized devices.

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
  \item
    \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark20}{}{}\textbf{OEM/OUI-Based Device
    Verification}
  \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
OEM/OUI-based identification is an additional essential method used in
the Rogue Device Detection System. The manufacturer of the network
interface card is identified by the OEM/OUI (Organizationally Unique
Identifier), which makes up the first 24 bits of a MAC address. The
system will determine whether a discovered device comes from a reliable
source by comparing the OUI with a list of reputable manufacturers.
{[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark176}{3}{]}
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Early Identification of Unrecognized Devices:} Devices with an
  OUI/OEM that does not match trusted manufacturers in the asset
  inventory are flagged as suspicious.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark21}{}{}This is very helpful in scenarios
where network policies restrict the use of equipment to those supplied
by known vendors.
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{MAC Spoofing Prevention:} While a rogue device may attempt to
  spoof its MAC address, if the OUI is inconsistent or does not align
  with authorized devices, the system can detect and raise an alert.
  This adds another critical layer of defense against unauthorized
  access. {[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark174}{1}{]}
  \end{quote}

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
  \item
    \begin{quote}
    \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark22}{}{}\textbf{Passive Monitoring with
    Promiscuous Mode}
    \end{quote}
  \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
Passive monitoring in promiscuous mode is a protective technique that
enables the system to examine all network traffic inside the local
subnet. When the network interface card (NIC) is in promiscuous mode, it
records and analyzes all network traffic, even if the packet's
destination MAC address doesn't match the NIC's. As a result, the system
can see every bit of communication, including the payload data,
protocols being used, and the source and destination MAC and IP
addresses {[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark177}{4}{]}.

Using this mode, the system gathers packets passively and examines them
for anomalies, including MAC address spoofing, unauthorized devices, and
odd traffic patterns. By verifying the OUI (Organizationally Unique
Identifier) component of MAC addresses with the manufacturer database
and cross-referencing packet sources with a whitelist of authorized MAC
addresses, it can identify spoofing. To find any rogue devices
exhibiting unusual behavior, like making a lot of ARP queries or spying,
the system also looks at traffic patterns.

Additionally, passive monitoring is a stealth method that helps map the
network topol- ogy by recognizing all active devices and their
communication patterns without creating extra traffic. In order to
verify device information and identify anomalies, it correlates packet
capture data with SNMP or LLDP protocol outputs, integrating smoothly
with the rogue detection system. An alert would be raised, for instance,
by a rogue device impersonating a genuine MAC address but acting in a
way that is not compatible with its OUI or expected function.

The system also creates alarms whenever it detects suspicious activity,
including mismatched MAC and IP addresses or unsafe network flows, and
logs all collected traffic for auditing purposes.
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Multicast and Broadcast Traffic:} Recording general network
  traffic aids in the identification of illegitimate devices that try to
  function covertly without making direct connections.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{ARP Requests:} By keeping an eye on ARP traffic, the system
  can identify odd trends that may point to efforts at man-in-the-middle
  (MITM) or ARP spoofing.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
The detection system is quite successful because of the passive
monitoring capacity that promiscuous mode offers, which guarantees
thorough visibility into all network activities and captures
communication that might not be explicitly directed to it.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark23}{}{}\textbf{Device Attribute
  Validation and Threshold Checking}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
Threshold validation is added to the whitelisting process for every
device that is identi- fied. Following a device's registration on the
whitelist, further characteristics including hostname, operating system,
and device type are checked against saved information. A possible rogue
device or suspicious activity may be indicated if any attribute deviates
from the established threshold. This guarantees that devices with
unusual features, even those that are valid, are detected and looked at.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark24}{}{}\textbf{Rogue Device Detection
  and Alerting}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
After the completion of all checks, a device is marked as a rogue device
if it fails the verification process. Network administrators are
informed of the existence of a possibly unauthorized device by real-time
alert generation. By continuously observing network traffic, the Rogue
Device Detection System maintains a current list of authorized devices
and flags any suspicious activity.
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Real-Time Alerts:} The system generates immediate alerts when
  a rogue device is detected, providing actionable information to
  network administrators for response.
\item
  \textbf{Logging for Analysis:} All detection events are logged,
  allowing for further inves- tigation and analysis of historical
  activity. This helps network administrators trace incidents and
  understand the timeline and nature of unauthorized access attempts.

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
  \item
    \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark25}{}{}\textbf{Experimentation
    Protocols for Validation}
  \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
Several essential protocols were used during experimentation to
determine the status of devices within the network, including ICMP,
Nmap, CDP, and LLDP:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{ICMP:} A layer-3 protocol used to determine if a device is
  responsive. Sending an ICMP packet to a device helps verify its
  operational status.
\item
  \textbf{Nmap:} Gathers detailed information regarding device
  connectivity and configura- tion. This includes probing IP addresses,
  MAC addresses, and discovering other identifying information about
  connected devices.
\item
  \textbf{CDP (Cisco Discovery Protocol) and LLDP (Link Layer Discovery
  Protocol):} Used to gather information about neighboring devices in
  the network. These protocols allow devices to share information such
  as device identity, IP address, and network
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark26}{}{}capabilities. In the Rogue Device
Detection System, CDP and LLDP are essential for building an accurate
understanding of the network topology, helping identify unknown devices
and their properties. This information aids in verifying whether devices
are legitimate or potentially rogue.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark27}{}{}\textbf{Existing Solutions for
  Rogue Device Detection}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
Several existing solutions aim to provide network access control and
rogue device detection. Among the popular tools are AnchorWatch, Cisco
Identity Services Engine (ISE), and Aruba ClearPass. Each solution
offers valuable features but also presents limitations in the context of
network security.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{AnchorWatch}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
AnchorWatch specializes in detecting rogue access points and
unauthorized devices within wireless networks. It continuously scans for
new devices and alerts administrators whenever a rogue device is
detected. While AnchorWatch is effective in Wi-Fi environments, it is
limited in addressing security for LAN networks. It lacks the capacity
for identifying unauthorized LAN devices and does not provide
comprehensive monitoring of all network activity.
{[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark178}{5}{]}
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Cisco Identity Services Engine (ISE)}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
Cisco Identity Services Engine (ISE) is a robust solution that provides
network access control across both LAN and wireless networks. Cisco ISE
includes:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Policy-Based Access Control:} By defining policies,
  administrators can manage which devices have access to the network and
  the resources they are allowed to use.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Comprehensive Device Profiling:} Cisco ISE uses detailed
  profiling techniques to classify devices based on type, operating
  system, and behavior. This allows the system to recognize devices that
  deviate from authorized profiles and respond accordingly.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Integration Capabilities:} Cisco ISE integrates seamlessly
  with other Cisco products to form a unified network security
  architecture {[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark179}{6}{]}.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
While Cisco ISE is powerful, it is also complex and expensive, making it
challenging for small to medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) to implement.
The high level of technical ex- pertise required to deploy and maintain
Cisco ISE makes it less accessible for organizations looking for a
simpler yet effective rogue detection solution.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{2}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark28}{}{}\textbf{Aruba ClearPass}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
Aruba ClearPass provides comprehensive network visibility and role-based
access control for both LAN and wireless environments. It offers:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{User-Friendly Interface:} The intuitive interface makes it
  easier for administrators to manage network access and respond to
  alerts.
\item
  \textbf{Real-Time Monitoring:} ClearPass continuously monitors network
  activity and generates alerts for any rogue devices or suspicious
  behavior.
\item
  \textbf{Role-Based Access Management:} It allows network
  administrators to define access levels for different devices,
  enhancing the security of sensitive resources.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
Despite its advantages, ClearPass can be overly complex for environments
focused primarily on LAN networks. Its features are extensive, but its
high cost and comprehensive role management capabilities may be beyond
what many organizations need for the specific purpose of rogue device
detection in a LAN.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark29}{}{}\textbf{Enhancements in Rogue
  Device Detection}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The Rogue Device Detection System aims to overcome the following
significant drawbacks of the already available tools which are used to
detect any unauthorized device.
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Verification Based on the Manufacturer:} OEM/OUI-based
  identification is not used by many applications to confirm the
  legitimacy of a connected device. The Rogue Device Detection system
  does this by examining each device's manufac- turer details, which
  provides a crucial security layer and aids in the more accurate
  identification of suspicious devices.
\item
  \textbf{Better Whitelist Verification:} This system has a Whitelist
  Verification Module that compares every connected device to a list of
  approved devices, in contrast to many other solutions now in use. This
  makes it much simpler to make sure that only reliable devices are
  linked, which improves the accuracy of rogue device detection.
\item
  \textbf{Promiscuous Mode Monitoring:} Promiscuous mode monitoring is
  included into the system to detect devices that passively record
  network data. By identifying sniffing devices that could otherwise go
  undetected, this function improves security and reduces the risk of
  tapping{[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark177}{4}{]}.
\item
  \textbf{Centralized Alert System:} While other tools might generate
  alerts, they often lack an organized system to manage these alerts
  effectively. In our system, all alerts are displayed on an
  Administrator Dashboard, making it easier for network admins to
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark30}{}{}see what's happening in real-time
and take quick action when an unauthorized device is detected.
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{User-Friendly Dashboard:} The Administrator Dashboard is
  simple and easy to use, making it much easier for network
  administrators to manage devices, view logs, and handle alerts---all
  from one place. Many existing tools don't offer such an intuitive and
  centralized interface, which can make managing network security more
  complicated.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Detailed Activity Logs:} The system also maintains a detailed
  Device Log Database that records all network activities and events.
  This allows administrators to look back, analyze historical data, and
  track unusual behavior over time. A lot of tools don't provide this
  level of detail, which can make it harder to identify long-term
  patterns or potential threats.
  \end{quote}

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
  \item
    \begin{quote}
    \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark31}{}{}\textbf{Rogue Device Detection
    System: Addressing the Gaps}
    \end{quote}
  \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
The Rogue Device Detection System integrates multiple advanced detection
methodologies to address these gaps, specifically for LAN
environments{[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark180}{7}{]}:
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Combination of Active and Passive Techniques}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The system uses both active scanning and passive monitoring to ensure a
comprehensive approach to rogue device detection. Nmap is employed for
active scanning to create a real-time inventory of devices, while
promiscuous mode enables the system to observe all network activity,
capturing devices that may attempt to remain hidden.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{OEM/OUI-Based Device Verification}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The system integrates OEM/OUI-based verification to identify devices by
their manufac- turers. By analyzing the first 24 bits of the MAC
address, the system can determine if a device belongs to a trusted OEM.
Devices with unrecognized or untrusted OUIs are flagged as potentially
rogue, allowing for early intervention. This verification method is
essential for identifying unauthorized consumer-grade devices that do
not meet enterprise security
requirements{[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark176}{3}{]}.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{2}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Promiscuous Mode and Traffic Analysis Using Scapy}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The integration of Wireshark for packet analysis further enhances the
effectiveness of the Rogue Device Detection System. The NIC is
configured in promiscuous mode to ensure that all packets on the network
segment are captured. Wireshark is then used to analyze the packets,
allowing for {[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark181}{8}{]}:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark32}{}{}\textbf{Detection of ARP Spoofing
  and Anomalies:} The system analyzes ARP traffic to identify and flag
  suspicious activities such as ARP spoofing.
\item
  \textbf{Detailed Manufacturer Analysis:} Captured packets are used to
  identify the OEM/OUI of each device, ensuring that devices are
  legitimate and belong to trusted manufactur- ers.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
The use of promiscuous mode enables the system to achieve complete
visibility into network activity, providing a level of insight that
purely active scanning methods cannot match.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \textbf{Promiscuous Mode as a Central Component}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
For the Rogue Device Detection System to be effective, promiscuous mode
is essential. By setting up the NIC to function in promiscuous mode, the
system can see the whole network and record each packet that moves over
the network segment.
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  Since stealth devices may try to function without actively delivering
  identifiable packets, they are detected via passive packet capture.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
Therefore, promiscuous mode offers a way to identify rogue devices that
might other- wise go undetected, thereby enhancing network security.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark33}{}{}\textbf{Future Enhancements: AI
  and Machine Learning}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
In order to enhance the detection and reaction capabilities of the Rogue
Device Detec- tion System, we intend to use artificial intelligence
(AI), machine learning (ML), and promiscuous mode monitoring in the
future:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Machine Learning for Behavioral Analysis:} The system can
  learn from the pre- vious actions of networked devices by utilizing
  machine learning. It will have the capacity to comprehend common
  patterns of device behavior and identify any anomalous activity that
  might point to the presence of a rogue device. This implies that
  without human assistance, the system can become more intelligent over
  time, adjusting to new threats and increasing its accuracy
  {[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark182}{9}{]}.
\item
  \textbf{Automated Responses:} With AI integration, the system will be
  able to handle threats automatically, such as isolating rogue devices
  or cutting off their access to the network as soon as they're
  detected. This will reduce the time needed to react to security
  issues, making sure rogue devices are dealt with immediately and
  minimizing any potential damage.

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
  \item
    \begin{quote}
    \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark34}{}{}\textbf{Comparison Table}
    \end{quote}
  \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

