\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark116}{}{}\textbf{Chapter 5}

\textbf{System Implementation}
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark117}{}{}\textbf{Introduction}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
System implementation is the phase in which the designed system is
translated into an operational one. In this chapter, the detailed
implementation process of the Rogue Device Detection System is
described. The development was carried out using the Django framework,
supported by PostgreSQL as the database backend, Django ORM for CRUD
operations, and HTML/CSS with Tailwind CSS for frontend styling.

The following sections elaborate on the framework configuration,
database setup, backend implementation, and frontend development. This
chapter also discusses the critical steps involved in integrating the
different modules and tools, ensuring the functionality is as expected
and fulfills the project objectives.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark118}{}{}\textbf{Development Framework
  and Tools}
  \end{quote}

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumii{\arabic{enumii}.}
  \item
    \begin{quote}
    \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark119}{}{}\textbf{Framework: Django}
    \end{quote}
  \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The entire backend of the system has been implemented using Django, a
high-level Python framework that facilitates rapid development and
clean, pragmatic design. Django was selected for this project due to its
following features:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Modular Structure:} Django offers a modular architecture,
  which is particularly beneficial for separating functionalities such
  as scanning, monitoring, logging, and administration.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Scalability:} The project requires efficient handling of
  network devices, making Django suitable for this task due to its
  inherent scalability.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Security:} Django's built-in security features, such as CSRF
  protection and SQL injection prevention, ensure that the system is
  robust and secure.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
37

The system was developed using Python as the primary programming
language due to its ease of readability and its comprehensive library
support, which proved crucial for network management and packet analysis
tasks.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark120}{}{}\textbf{Database: PostgreSQL}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The database backend for the project is PostgreSQL, an open-source
object-relational database system that is known for its robustness,
performance, and ACID compliance. PostgreSQL was chosen for the
following reasons:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Support for Complex Queries:} PostgreSQL allows for advanced
  querying, mak- ing it suitable for the complex filtering required when
  verifying devices against a whitelist.
\item
  \textbf{Compatibility with Django ORM:} Django integrates seamlessly
  with PostgreSQL through Django ORM, which offers the ability to
  interact with the database using Python code, providing a more
  abstract and easier-to-understand data model.
\end{itemize}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{2}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark121}{}{}\textbf{Frontend Development}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The frontend of the system is built using a combination of HTML, CSS,
and Tailwind CSS:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{HTML and CSS:} Used for creating the basic structure and
  styling of the user interface.
\item
  \textbf{Tailwind CSS:} Provides additional, responsive styling that
  aligns with modern web design standards, improving the overall user
  experience.
\end{itemize}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{3}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark122}{}{}\textbf{CRUD Operations with
  Django ORM}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
For interacting with the database, CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete)
operations were implemented using Django ORM:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Create:} Add a new device to the whitelist, register a rogue
  device, or add log entries.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \textbf{Read:} Query devices from the whitelist, fetch log entries for
  specific actions, or get alert history.
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Update:} Update details of registered devices or modify
  whitelist entries.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Delete:} Remove rogue devices or outdated log entries.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
Django's ORM is leveraged for defining database models, which provided
an efficient way to work with the database through Python code,
minimizing SQL exposure and reducing errors.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{2}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark123}{}{}\textbf{Detailed Module
  Descriptions}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The Rogue Device Detection System has been implemented using a modular
approach to ensure scalability, easier maintenance, and clear separation
of responsibilities. The following are the key modules that have been
implemented in the project:
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark124}{}{}\textbf{Network Scanning Module}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The Network Scanning Module is the core of the system's detection
mechanism. It scans the entire LAN network to identify all devices
currently connected. The module uses Nmap, a popular network scanning
tool, to discover devices, their IP addresses, MAC addresses, and open
ports.

\textbf{Features and Implementation}
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Network Discovery:} Uses Nmap to identify active devices on
  the LAN and gathers information such as IP address, MAC address, and
  Hostname.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Automation:} Integrated into Django views, this module allows
  the Network Admin- istrator to start network scans directly from the
  web interface.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Result Compilation:} The data collected from scanning is
  compiled and saved in the Network Devices table, which contains
  detailed information about each detected device.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Scheduling:} Using Django's Celery for background tasks,
  network scans can be scheduled at regular intervals to continuously
  keep the list of connected devices up-to-date.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Role in the System}

The Network Scanning Module is the entry point for all other modules. It
ensures that an accurate list of network devices is always available,
serving as the foundation for whitelist verification and further device
analysis.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark125}{}{}\textbf{Whitelist Verification
  Module}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The Whitelist Verification Module is responsible for checking if a
detected device is authorized to be on the network. This module compares
the results from the Network Scanning Module against the Whitelist
Database, which contains the MAC addresses of all authorized devices.

\textbf{Features and Implementation}
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Whitelist Comparison:} For every new device discovered by the
  Network Scanning Module, the Whitelist Verification Module checks the
  MAC address against the Whitelist Database.
\item
  \textbf{Whitelist Management:} Through the Administrator Dashboard,
  the Network Ad- ministrator can add, update, or remove devices from
  the whitelist. This ensures that only authorized devices are on the
  network.
\item
  \textbf{Database Interaction:} This module uses Django ORM to
  efficiently query the Whitelist Database, allowing authorized devices
  to be marked as safe, while uniden- tified devices are flagged for
  further inspection.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Role in the System}

The Whitelist Verification Module ensures that only authorized devices
are allowed on the network. It acts as the first line of defense against
rogue devices by maintaining a list of all known and permitted devices.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{2}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark126}{}{}\textbf{OEM/OUI Identification
  Module}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The OEM/OUI Identification Module is used to identify the manufacturer
of the detected devices. It does this by analyzing the Organizationally
Unique Identifier (OUI), which is the first part of the MAC address.

\textbf{Features and Implementation}
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Manufacturer Identification:} By checking the OUI portion of
  the MAC address, this module identifies the manufacturer of each
  device. This helps validate if the device should be on the network.
\item
  \textbf{Suspicious Manufacturer Detection:} If a device is from an
  unexpected or suspicious manufacturer, the system flags it for further
  investigation.
\item
  \textbf{Database Storage:} The identified manufacturer information is
  stored in the OEM/OUI Identification Database, which can later be used
  for further validation.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Role in the System}

The OEM/OUI Identification Module helps identify potentially rogue
devices based on their manufacturer. If a device belongs to a suspicious
or unrecognized manufacturer, the system will flag it as potentially
malicious.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{3}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark127}{}{\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark128}{}{}}\textbf{Promiscuous
  Mode Monitoring Module}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The Promiscuous Mode Monitoring Module is responsible for monitoring
network packets to identify any devices operating in promiscuous mode.
Devices in promiscuous mode can eavesdrop on network traffic, which
could signify a rogue or malicious actor
{[}\protect\hyperlink{_bookmark183}{10}{]}.

\textbf{Features and Implementation}
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Traffic Monitoring:} Using Wireshark integrated through Python
  scripts, this module captures packets and identifies devices
  attempting to read all traffic on the network.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Detection of Suspicious Behavior:} If a device is detected in
  promiscuous mode, it is flagged as suspicious and logged in the Device
  Log Database.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Integration with Alert System:} The module integrates with the
  Alert System to notify the Network Administrator whenever promiscuous
  mode is detected.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Role in the System}

This module acts as a proactive defense mechanism, helping detect
potential eavesdroppers that may pose a risk to network security.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{4}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark129}{}{}\textbf{Alert System Module}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The Alert System Module generates alerts whenever suspicious devices are
detected, whether through Whitelist Verification or OEM Identification.

\textbf{Features and Implementation}
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Real-Time Alerts:} Alerts are generated and sent in real-time.
  Alerts can be delivered via email to the Network Administrator for
  immediate response.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Dashboard Notifications:} Alerts are also displayed on the
  Administrator Dashboard for review, making them easily accessible at
  all times.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Severity Levels:} Alerts are categorized based on severity
  levels:
  \end{quote}

  \begin{itemize}
  \item
    \begin{quote}
    \textbf{Low Priority:} Non-critical issues (e.g., new unknown
    device).
    \end{quote}
  \item
    \begin{quote}
    \textbf{High Priority:} Immediate attention needed (e.g., detection
    of a potentially malicious device).
    \end{quote}
  \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Role in the System}

The Alert System Module ensures that the Network Administrator is
promptly informed of any suspicious activities, allowing for quick
action to secure the network.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{5}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark130}{}{}\textbf{Device Log Database
  Module}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The Device Log Database Module records all network events, including new
device detections, whitelist verifications, and any alerts raised by the
system.

\textbf{Features and Implementation}
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Comprehensive Logging:} All events, from scans to
  administrative actions, are logged for accountability and
  traceability.
\item
  \textbf{Time-Stamped Entries:} Logs are timestamped, ensuring that all
  activities can be traced back to the exact moment they occurred.
\item
  \textbf{Report Generation:} The Administrator Dashboard provides
  options for generating reports based on logged activities, which can
  be used for auditing and analysis.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Role in the System}

The Device Log Database Module provides a record of all system
activities, which is crucial for monitoring, auditing, and
troubleshooting purposes. The logs are also used for compliance and
forensic analysis in case of a security incident.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{6}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark131}{}{}\textbf{Administrator Dashboard
  Module}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The Administrator Dashboard Module is the control center of the Rogue
Device Detection System, providing the Network Administrator with a
user-friendly interface to monitor the network, view alerts, manage the
whitelist, and generate reports.

\textbf{Features and Implementation}
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Whitelist Management:} The administrator can add, update, or
  remove devices from the whitelist, ensuring only trusted devices are
  allowed on the network.
\item
  \textbf{Alerts and Notifications:} Displays all current alerts and
  their status, allowing the administrator to act on them effectively.
\item
  \textbf{Logs and Reports:} Provides access to the Device Log Database
  and allows for the generation of custom reports based on criteria such
  as date ranges, alert severity, etc.
\item
  \textbf{Responsive Design:} Implemented using HTML, CSS, and Tailwind
  CSS, the dash- board is responsive and accessible across multiple
  devices.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Role in the System}

The Administrator Dashboard Module provides a centralized view of the
system, making it easier for the Network Administrator to keep track of
all activities, manage devices, and address any security concerns.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{7}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark132}{}{}\textbf{CRUD Operations Module}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The CRUD Operations Module is implemented using Django ORM to handle all
database interactions. CRUD operations are central to managing device
records, whitelist entries, and logs.

\textbf{Features and Implementation}
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Create:} Enables the addition of new devices to the Whitelist
  Database, creation of new log entries, and registration of detected
  rogue devices.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Read:} Allows the administrator to query device details,
  whitelist entries, and histori- cal logs from the PostgreSQL Database.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Update:} Used for modifying existing whitelist entries or
  updating device information after verification.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Delete:} Outdated log entries and unauthorized devices can be
  deleted using this module, keeping the database clean and relevant.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Role in the System}

This module is critical for effective data management within the Rogue
Device Detection System. By abstracting the database operations using
Django ORM, the system ensures a cleaner and safer interaction with the
database.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{8}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark133}{}{}\textbf{Report Generation
  Module}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The Report Generation Module provides network administrators with the
ability to generate customized reports that help in understanding
network activity, identifying patterns, and reviewing historical
incidents.

\textbf{Features and Implementation}
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Custom Reports:} Reports can be generated for any activity
  logged by the Device Log Database. Reports may include alerts raised,
  devices verified, and actions taken.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \textbf{Filter Options:} Reports can be filtered based on various
  attributes such as date range, alert type, or severity level.
\item
  \textbf{Exportable Format:} Reports can be exported as PDF or CSV for
  sharing or record- keeping purposes.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Role in the System}

The Report Generation Module helps the Network Administrator gain
insights into the network's status over time. It provides data to
analyze trends and proactively strengthen the network's defenses against
rogue devices.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{9}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark134}{}{}\textbf{Database Integration}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The PostgreSQL Database is an integral part of the system, designed to
efficiently manage and store information:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Whitelist Database:} Contains entries for authorized devices.
  Django models define the structure, and ORM ensures easy interaction.
\item
  \textbf{Device Log Database:} Stores detailed information about each
  log entry, including scans, alerts, and administrator actions.
\item
  \textbf{Alert System Database:} Stores all the generated alerts,
  including details about rogue devices and the actions taken.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
The use of Django migrations ensured that changes in the models were
consistently reflected in the PostgreSQL database schema.
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{10}
\item
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark135}{}{}\textbf{Deployment}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The deployment of the Rogue Device Detection System involves:
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  \textbf{Hosting:} The system was deployed on an internal server, with
  PostgreSQL config- ured to be accessed securely.
\item
  \textbf{Web Server Configuration:} Gunicorn was used as the WSGI
  server, and Nginx acted as the reverse proxy to handle incoming
  requests.
\item
  \textbf{Static Files Management:} Static files (HTML, CSS, JavaScript)
  were collected and served using Django's collectstatic command.
  Tailwind CSS was used for styling, enhancing the responsiveness and
  aesthetics of the user interface.
\item
  \textbf{Network Tools Integration:} The network scanning tools, such
  as Nmap, were installed on the server, and their execution was
  integrated with Django views to allow network scanning directly from
  the web interface.

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumii{\arabic{enumii}.}
  \item
    \begin{quote}
    \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark136}{}{}\textbf{Testing}
    \end{quote}
  \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
The implementation was tested in multiple phases:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Unit Testing:} Django's built-in testing tools were used to
  perform unit testing on individual modules such as the Whitelist
  Verification Module and Alert Generation Module.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Integration Testing:} Tested the integration between different
  components such as the Network Scanning Module, Whitelist Verification
  Module, and Alert System.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{User Acceptance Testing (UAT):} The Network Administrator
  tested the system to ensure that all functionalities, such as viewing
  alerts, managing the whitelist, and generating reports, worked as
  expected.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark137}{}{}\textbf{Challenges and
  Solutions}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
During implementation, several challenges were faced:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Network Complexity:} Integrating different network scanning
  and monitoring tools, such as Nmap, into a web-based system required
  careful handling of permissions and ensuring the security of sensitive
  operations.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Solution:} The scanning operations were isolated and given
restricted permissions to ensure that rogue actors could not misuse the
tool.
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Database Optimization:} Ensuring that the PostgreSQL database
  handled high- frequency queries efficiently was challenging.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Solution:} Indexes were created on frequently queried fields,
and Django ORM's query optimizations were used to reduce database load.

\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark138}{}{}\textbf{Chapter 6}

\textbf{System Testing and Evaluation}
\end{quote}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
