  \begin{quote}
  \protect\hypertarget{_bookmark160}{}{}\textbf{User Feedback}
  \end{quote}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{quote}
User acceptance testing revealed:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Ease of Use}: Administrators found the dashboard intuitive and
  effective for managing devices.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Alert Relevance}: Alerts provided actionable information about
  rogue devices.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Overall Satisfaction}: Users appreciated the accuracy and
  responsiveness of the system.
  \end{quote}
\end{itemize}

\hypertarget{testing-results}{%
\subsection{Testing Results}\label{testing-results}}

\begin{quote}
The testing phase demonstrated the effectiveness of the Rogue Device
Detection System in detecting and managing rogue devices. Highlights
include:
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \begin{quote}
  \textbf{Detection Accuracy}: Achieved 97\% accuracy in identifying
  rogue devices.
  \end{quote}
\item
  \textbf{Response Time}: Average response time was less than 5 seconds
  for detecting rogue devices and generating alerts.
\item
  \textbf{Scalability}: Successfully managed up to 1000 devices with
  minor database optimiza- tions.
\item
  \textbf{User Satisfaction}: Positive feedback from network
  administrators during user ac- ceptance testing.

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
  \item ~
    \hypertarget{challenges-and-improvements}{%
    \subsection{Challenges and
    Improvements}\label{challenges-and-improvements}}

    \begin{enumerate}
    \def\labelenumii{\arabic{enumii}.}
    \item ~
      \hypertarget{challenges}{%
      \subsubsection{Challenges}\label{challenges}}

      \begin{itemize}
      \item
        \textbf{Network Simulation Complexity}: Configuring realistic
        network environments in GNS3 was challenging, particularly for
        large-scale networks.
      \item
        \textbf{Database Optimization}: Ensuring PostgreSQL handled
        high-frequency write opera- tions efficiently was difficult,
        especially during heavy load scenarios.
      \end{itemize}
    \item ~
      \hypertarget{improvements}{%
      \subsubsection{Improvements}\label{improvements}}

      \begin{itemize}
      \item
        \textbf{Database Indexing}: Added indexing on frequently queried
        fields to improve query performance.
      \item
        \textbf{Dynamic Threshold Alerts}: Implemented dynamic
        thresholds to reduce false posi- tives, enhancing accuracy.
      \item
        \textbf{User Interface Enhancements}: Improved dashboard
        navigation and added visual indicators for alert severity based
        on user feedback.
      \end{itemize}
    \end{enumerate}
  \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
\protect\hypertarget{_bookmark165}{}{}\textbf{Chapter 7}

\textbf{Conclusion and Future Work}
\end{quote}

\hypertarget{conclusion}{%
\subsection{Conclusion}\label{conclusion}}

\begin{quote}
The Rogue Device Detection System represents a significant advancement
in network security for LAN environments. By combining network scanning,
whitelist verification, and manufacturer identification, the system
provides a comprehensive approach to detecting and managing rogue
devices.

The use of modern tools such as Django, PostgreSQL, Wireshark, and GNS3
allowed for the implementation of a robust, efficient, and scalable
system. The modular nature of the system ensures that it is easy to
maintain, extend, and adapt to future needs.

Testing and evaluation have shown that the system performs well under a
variety of network conditions, effectively identifying rogue devices and
alerting administrators in a timely manner. The implementation of the
Administrator Dashboard made managing devices, verifying alerts, and
reviewing logs simple and intuitive, empowering the network
administrator to maintain full control over the network.

Moving forward, the system can be enhanced with the integration of
machine learning, improved scalability features, and better user
interaction tools, including mobile applica- tions. These enhancements
will ensure that the Rogue Device Detection System remains at the
forefront of network security solutions for LAN environments, capable of
adapting to evolving threats and ensuring that the integrity of the
network is always maintained.

In conclusion, the project has successfully achieved its objectives of
developing an effective rogue device detection system. It has created a
pathway for more advanced tools and systems that will further secure
network environments in an increasingly connected world. With the
continued development of these technologies, network administrators will
have even more powerful tools at their disposal to protect critical
infrastructure from unauthorized devices.

53
\end{quote}

\hypertarget{future-work}{%
\subsection{Future Work}\label{future-work}}

\begin{quote}
There are several areas in which the system could be further improved or
extended in future iterations:
\end{quote}

\hypertarget{machine-learning-integration}{%
\subsubsection{Machine Learning
Integration}\label{machine-learning-integration}}

\begin{quote}
The system can be enhanced with machine learning models that learn from
network traffic data and device behavior patterns to detect anomalies.
This would enable the system to identify rogue devices more proactively
and reduce false positives.
\end{quote}

\hypertarget{improved-scalability-for-larger-networks}{%
\subsubsection{Improved Scalability for Larger
Networks}\label{improved-scalability-for-larger-networks}}

\begin{quote}
The current system has been tested with network sizes up to 1000
devices. However, further work can be done to ensure that the system
remains performant and effective for much larger networks, such as
corporate campuses or industrial facilities.
\end{quote}

\hypertarget{automated-whitelist-management}{%
\subsubsection{Automated Whitelist
Management}\label{automated-whitelist-management}}

\begin{quote}
The Whitelist Verification Module could be improved with automated
whitelist manage- ment. This would involve dynamic learning of devices
over time, allowing for easier addition of legitimate devices and
improved response to rogue device alerts.
\end{quote}

\hypertarget{mobile-application-for-administrator-alerts}{%
\subsubsection{Mobile Application for Administrator
Alerts}\label{mobile-application-for-administrator-alerts}}

\begin{quote}
Developing a mobile application for network administrators would enhance
the responsive- ness of the system by allowing alerts and notifications
to be received directly on a mobile device. This would make it easier
for administrators to respond quickly to threats, even when away from
their workstations.
\end{quote}

\hypertarget{integration-with-other-security-tools}{%
\subsubsection{Integration with Other Security
Tools}\label{integration-with-other-security-tools}}

\begin{quote}
Future development could also involve integrating the system with other
network security tools, such as Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS),
firewalls, and SIEM platforms. This would enable a more holistic
approach to network security.
\end{quote}

